WEBVTT

00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:01.133
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 1

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:05.510
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> …and having fewer people in the office may simply not be an option,

00:00:05.884 --> 00:00:09.239
not to mention that many employees may not want a drop in salary

00:00:09.405 --> 00:00:11.292
in exchange for a shorter working week.

00:00:13.365 --> 00:00:14.038
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 2

00:00:14.832 --> 00:00:15.718
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> In particular,

00:00:16.051 --> 00:00:19.739
people who were religious about taking short breaks were far more productive

00:00:19.775 --> 00:00:21.365
than those who worked longer hours.

00:00:23.395 --> 00:00:24.185
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 3

00:00:24.915 --> 00:00:29.614
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> They discovered that the ideal work-to-break ratio was 52 minutes of work,

00:00:29.876 --> 00:00:32.250
followed by 18 minutes of rest.

00:00:34.249 --> 00:00:34.952
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 4

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:37.817
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> Apart from getting you into the right rhythm,

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:42.538
planning your day around hour-long intervals simplifies daunting tasks

00:00:42.702 --> 00:00:44.828
by breaking them into manageable chunks.

00:00:46.724 --> 00:00:47.493
<v><b>Narrator:</b></v> 5

00:00:48.365 --> 00:00:51.215
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> If you want to follow the research recommendations to the letter,

00:00:51.585 --> 00:00:56.329
you can plan your day around 52-minute intervals if you like, but an hour works just as well.